{
  "abstract": "A connected graph G on n vertices is 3-good if its Ramsey number against the triangle, R(K_3, G), equals 2n - 1, the smallest possible value. Following Burr, Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau and Schelp, let f(n) be the largest number of edges of a connected 3-good graph on n vertices, and let F(n) be the largest q such that every connected graph on n vertices with at most q edges is 3-good. Erdős Problem 1182 asks to estimate f(n) and F(n) and whether F(n)/n tends to infinity. This formalization proves, for the statements of the problem in Formal Conjectures, that f(n) = Θ(n^{3/2} (log n)^{1/2}), that F(n) = Θ(n), and that F(n)/n does not tend to infinity, together with five supporting statements (the trivial bound R(K_3, G) ≥ 2n - 1, Chvátal's theorem for trees and K_3, F(n) ≥ n - 1, and the two bounds for f(n) of Burr, Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau and Schelp). The lower bound for f(n) and all results about F(n) are known. The upper bound f(n) = O(n^{3/2} (log n)^{1/2}) was not in the refereed literature when the proof was written; the best published bound was O(n^{3/2} log n), from a theorem of Sudakov. It is proved by following the triangle-free process on K_N for about N^{3/2} (log N)^{1/2} steps, with all concentration estimates proved in Lean. The proofs were produced by an AI system and have not been reviewed by a human.",
